# Line endings — CI runs on Linux, so executable text must be checked out LF.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Every shell script in this repository is executed by a Linux
# runner (ci.yml) or inside a Linux container (the qe-explorer / qe-central
# images, scripts/qec_ci_db_setup.sh, ci/run_*_tests.sh). Bash cannot parse a
# CRLF script: it reads the carriage return as part of the last token and fails
# with a syntax error that names a line which is visibly correct —
#
#     scripts/gate_rollback.sh: line 33: syntax error near unexpected token $'in\r'
#     scripts/gate_rollback.sh: line 33: `  case "$1" in'
#
# — which is exactly as confusing to debug as it looks. Observed on this repo:
# tests/deploy_gate reported "12 failed, 11 errors" against a CRLF working tree
# and "116 passed" against the identical files normalised to LF. Nothing was
# wrong with the code.
#
# Without this file the outcome depends on each developer's `core.autocrlf`.
# That happens to be `true` on the current Windows checkout, so its commits are
# normalised to LF and CI is fine today. A contributor with `core.autocrlf=false`
# would commit CRLF and break every shell-script job on Linux — a repository-wide
# breakage caused by a local git setting nobody looks at.
#
# Scoped deliberately to the file types where the line ending is FUNCTIONAL
# rather than cosmetic, instead of a blanket `* text=auto` that would rewrite
# every file in the tree as a side effect of a CI fix.
*.sh      text eol=lf
*.bash    text eol=lf

# Interpreter shebang lines have the same problem as bash: a `#!/usr/bin/env
# python3\r` is not a valid interpreter path. Python source is also read by
# tools (ruff, pytest assertion rewriting) that report CR as content.
*.py      text eol=lf

# Consumed by Linux tooling: docker build, alembic, helm, kubectl, GitHub Actions.
Dockerfile* text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.sql     text eol=lf

# PowerShell is run by Windows hosts (scripts/deploy.ps1 on the operator's box),
# so it keeps CRLF rather than being dragged into the Linux convention.
*.ps1     text eol=crlf

# Binary — never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.gz      binary
*.tgz     binary
*.zip     binary
